## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Reagents and Instruments:
Both the silica powder (100-200 mesh) for column chromatography and the thin layer chromatographic sheets (60 F254) for TLC were purchased from Qingdao Ocean Chemicals (Qingdao, China). NMR spectra were conducted by using Bruker DRX-400 spectrometer with tetramethylsilane (TMS) as an internal standard. All chemical shifts were reported in the standard δ notation of parts per million. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data was collected on Rigaku Miniflex II diffactometer at $30\mathrm{kV}$ , $15~\mathrm{mA}$ for Cu Ka $(1=1.5418\mathrm{\AA})$ , with a scan speed of $5^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ and a step size of $0.05^{\circ}$ in 2θ at room temperature. One-photon fluorescent experiments were conducted on a Hitachi-F4500 fluorometer with both excitation and emission slits of $5.0\mathrm{nm}$ . Two-photon fluorescence studies were carried out by employing a mode-locked Ti:sapphire pulsed laser (Chameleon Ultra II, Coherent Inc.) as excitation and a DCS200PC single photon counting (Beijing Zolix Instruments Co., Ltd.) as collector. Two-photon fluorescence images were obtained using an Olympus FV1000-MPE multiphoton laser scanning confocal microscope (Japan).
## Preparation of TP-MOF Probes
! CAUTION: Azide compounds are potentially explosive, and also display significant human toxicity. Be extremely careful when heating of these compounds. Wear goggles and gloves, and perform the experiment in a fume hood.
PCN-58 was synthesized according to literature protocols30 with only minor modifications. In brief, $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(24~\mathrm{mg})$ and TPDC derivative dicarboxylic acid ligand (synthesis details in SI) $\mathrm{30~mg)}$ were ultrasonically dissolved in DMF $(3.0~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and heated to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ in an oil-bath pan. After cooling down to room temperature, the resulting light yellow solid was centrifuged and stirred with absolute ethanol for 3 days at room temperature before being centrifuged again, then washed with ethanol three times, before being dried under vacuum.
Next, the synthesized MOFs were modified with the organic probes through the $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ -catalyzed azide-alkyne cycloaddition protocol (CuAAC). PCN-58 ( $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ in DMF $\{5\mathrm{mL}\}$ ) was reacted with organic moiety for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , named as alkynyl-BR- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ (52 mg) (synthesis details in Supporting Information) under nitrogen and in the presence of CuI $\langle5\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{h}$ . The product was centrifuged and washed with DMF and ethanol, and then dried in vacuum. The amino group of this material (PCN-58-BR$\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ ) was then turned into azide group by use of $t{\mathrm{.}}$ -BuONO and $\mathrm{TMSN}_{3}$ to approach the synthesis of probe 1. Probe 2 was prepared by reacting PCN-58 ( $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ in DMF (5 mL) with organic moiety for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , named as alkynyl-DL $(70~\mathrm{mg})$ (synthesis details in Supporting Information) under nitrogen and in the presence of CuI $(3~\mathrm{mg})$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was centrifuged and washed with DMF and ethanol, and then dried in vacuum, thus obtaining probe 2. Part of two probes were separately dispersed in water with HF and kept at room temperature for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to decompose. The suspension was filtered and washed with water $200\mathrm{mL})$ , and then the residue was dried in vacuum for NMR spectra record.
## In vitro detecting of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ and $\mathbf{Z_{n}}^{2+}$
To study the performance of TP-MOF as nanoprobes for fluorescent molecular sensing, the probe was dispersed in $1\times{\overrightarrow{\mathbf{\alpha}}}$ HEPES (10 mM, 150 mM NaCl, pH 7.4). A
series of NaHS or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ solutions at rising concentrations were added to dispersions of probe 1 or probe 2 $\mathrm{100~\upmug/mL)}$ accordingly. The resulting mixtures were then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ before being analyzed by fluorescence spectroscopy.
## Biostability in serum/cell culture
The TP-MOF probe was dispersed in fetal bovine serum (FBS, HyClone, UT) or high-glucose Dulbecco’s modified eagle’s medium (DMEM, HyClone, UT), with a final concentration of $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ . These dispersions were kept at room temperature for 72 h. Subsequently, the probe was collected as a powder via centrifugation $(13000\mathrm{rpm}$ , 5 min, RT) and washed with water 3 times, then dried under vacuum, before being stored at room temperature in a desiccator prior to PXRD analysis.
## Cytotoxicity assay
An MTS assay was performed. First, HeLa cells were seeded in a 96-well plate at a density of $5\times10^{3}$ per well and incubated with the probe at concentrations ranging from 10 to $200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the incubation, the medium containing probes was removed, then fresh culture medium $(100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was supplied. The cell viability was measured by adding $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of MTS solution to each well followed by incubation of 0.5h, then the UV absorbance of MTS at 490 nm was recorded with a microplate reader.
## Intracellular sensing of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ and $\mathbf{Z_{n}}^{2+}$
For intracellular sensing of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , HeLa cells were first seeded on glass-bottom culture dishes (Nest, China) for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Probe 1 ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $10~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) was mixed with $990~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMEM and then incubated with HeLa cells for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The cells were then washed 3 times with DPBS and used for TPM imaging at $820~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation wavelength. HeLa cells were first incubated with probe 1, then further incubated with NaHS $(200~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for 30 min. These same experimental conditions were also applied to incubation with A549 and HepG2 cells. In the case of probe 2, TPM imaging for presence of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in live cells was investigated. HeLa cells were seeded on glass-bottom culture dishes, and first incubated with probe 2 ( $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , then with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}(20\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and pyrithione (40 $\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, the cells were washed with DPBS and then incubated with DMEM for TPM imaging at $820\mathrm{nm}$ excitation wavelength.
## Imaging of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ and $\mathbf{Z_{n}}^{2+}$ in tissue slice
For imaging in tissue slices of TP-MOF probes, frozen rat liver or lung tissue slices were thawed at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with wet cotton, then incubated with probe 1 ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL},$ for 6 h, respectively. They were further incubated with NaHS $(200~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Imaging of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in rat liver tissue slices was explored by first incubating with probe 2 ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ for 6 h, then further incubating with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ $20~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Subsequently, the tissue slices were washed with DPBS for TPM imaging at $820\mathrm{nm}$ excitation wavelength.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Characterization of TP-MOF probes
Morphology of TP-MOF probes were confirmed through transmission electron microscope (TEM) with results shown in Figure 1A and 1D, presenting a structured morphology with an average size of $200\mathrm{nm}$ . The structural identity of TP-MOF probes with naked MOFs were confirmed through powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure 1B and Figure 1E). Similar peaks of PXRD pattern between TP-MOF probes and naked MOFs demonstrated the well preserved morphologies and crystalline structures of TP-MOFs inherited from MOF building blocks without significant changes as a consequence of their chemical modifications. Besides, UV-vis absorbance spectra (Figure 1C and 1F) demonstrate successful conjugation of TP organic probes with MOFs.
